"""English — details, faith (52 questions)."""

D = {
"faith_019": "The verse belongs to Surah Al-Ma'idah: \"Indeed, We sent down the Tawrat, in which was guidance and light. By it the prophets who submitted to Allah judged for the Jews, as did the rabbis and the learned\" (5:44). Two verses later 'Isa, son of Maryam, comes confirming the Tawrat before him (5:46), and elsewhere the Qur'an says plainly, \"We gave Musa the Book\" (2:87). A Muslim honours the Tawrat as a true revelation from Allah, given to Musa before the Qur'an.",
"faith_020": "Surah Al-Ma'idah places 'Isa in the line of the prophets before him: \"And We sent, following in their footsteps, 'Isa son of Maryam, confirming what came before him of the Tawrat; and We gave him the Injil, in which was guidance and light\" (5:46). The next verse tells the people of the Injil to judge by what Allah revealed in it (5:47). Each Book confirms the one before it, and a Muslim believes in the Injil given to 'Isa as one link in that chain of revelation.",
"faith_021": "The verse comes in Surah An-Nisa: \"And when you are greeted with a greeting, greet with one better than it or return it. Indeed, Allah is ever, over all things, an Accountant\" (4:86). The greeting the Qur'an teaches is one of peace: believers entering houses \"greet one another with a greeting from Allah, blessed and good\" (24:61), and the greeting of the people of Paradise is \"Peace\" (10:10). A Muslim answers every salam at least as warmly as it was given, knowing that Allah counts even this small courtesy.",
"faith_022": "Surah Al-A'la closes with a contrast: \"He has succeeded who purifies himself, and remembers the name of his Lord and prays. But you prefer the life of this world, while the Hereafter is better and more lasting\" (87:14-17). Then: \"Indeed, this is in the former Scrolls, the Scrolls of Ibrahim and Musa\" (87:18-19). Surah An-Najm also names the Scrolls of Musa and Ibrahim (53:36-37). A Muslim learns that the call to purify oneself, pray and prefer the Hereafter was revealed to prophets long before Muhammad ﷺ.",
"faith_023": "Surah Az-Zukhruf shows the wrongdoers in Hell, their punishment never lightened (43:74-75). Then comes their cry: \"And they will call, 'O Malik, let your Lord put an end to us!' He will say, 'Indeed, you will remain'\" (43:77). The Qur'an also says that over the Fire are angels, stern and severe, who never disobey what Allah commands them (66:6). A Muslim learns that the angels carry out Allah's justice exactly, and that the time to seek His mercy is now.",
"faith_024": "Surah Al-Haqqa describes the Day: the Trumpet is blown, earth and mountains are crushed, and the sky splits (69:13-16). Then: \"And the angels are at its edges. And there will bear the Throne of your Lord above them, that Day, eight\" (69:17). Surah Ghafir adds that those who carry the Throne glorify their Lord with praise, believe in Him, and ask forgiveness for the believers (40:7). A Muslim learns that the angels nearest the Throne pray for those who believe, and takes comfort in it.",
"faith_025": "Surah As-Sajdah answers those who doubted the resurrection (32:10): \"Say: The angel of death, who has been put in charge of you, will take your souls. Then to your Lord you will be returned\" (32:11). The next verse shows the wrongdoers begging to be sent back to do good (32:12). Elsewhere the Qur'an says that Allah's messengers take the soul \"and they do not fail\" (6:61). A Muslim learns that death comes exactly at its time, and that what follows it is the return to Allah.",
"faith_026": "The hadith is narrated by 'A'isha in Sahih Muslim 2996: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the angels were created from light, the jinn from a smokeless flame of fire, and Adam from what had been described to us. \"What has been described\" points to the Qur'an: man from clay (55:14), the jinn from a smokeless flame of fire (55:15). Iblis himself said, \"You created me from fire and created him from clay\" (7:12). A Muslim learns that angels, jinn and humans are three creations of one Creator.",
"faith_027": "The verse belongs to Surah Ar-Rahman, which lists the favours of Allah: \"He created man from clay like pottery\" (55:14), \"and He created the jinn from a smokeless flame of fire\" (55:15), followed by the refrain, \"So which of the favours of your Lord will you both deny?\" (55:16). Iblis, who was of the jinn (18:50), argued from this origin when he refused to bow to Adam (7:12). A Muslim learns that the jinn are real creatures, unseen but created, and that Allah alone made them and us.",
"faith_028": "The verse belongs to Surah Fatir: \"Indeed, Satan is an enemy to you; so take him as an enemy\" (35:6). The enmity began when Iblis, refusing to bow to Adam, vowed to lie in wait for people on the straight path (7:16-17). The defence is given too: \"When you recite the Qur'an, seek refuge in Allah from Satan, the expelled\" (16:98). A Muslim learns to treat every whisper toward evil as an enemy's, and to seek refuge in Allah from it.",
"faith_029": "Surah Al-Ahqaf ends with Allah addressing the Prophet ﷺ: \"So be patient, as the messengers of firm resolve were patient, and do not seek to hasten it for them\" (46:35). The verse does not list them; Surah Ash-Shura names the religion enjoined upon Nuh, revealed to Muhammad ﷺ, and enjoined upon Ibrahim, Musa and 'Isa (42:13). Surah Al-Ahzab names the same five in the covenant taken from the prophets (33:7). A Muslim learns that these five carried the heaviest missions, and that their patience is the model.",
"faith_030": "The verse comes near the end of Surah Az-Zumar: \"And the Trumpet will be blown, and whoever is in the heavens and whoever is on the earth will fall dead, except whom Allah wills. Then it will be blown again, and at once they will be standing, looking on\" (39:68). The next verse shows the earth shining with its Lord's light and the record laid open (39:69). A Muslim learns that there are two blasts, one that ends this world and one that raises everyone for judgement.",
"faith_031": "The verse belongs to Surah Al-Anbiya: \"And We will set up the scales of justice for the Day of Resurrection, so no soul will be wronged at all. And if there is even the weight of a mustard seed, We will bring it forth\" (21:47). Surah Al-Mu'minun tells what they decide: \"Those whose scales are heavy are the successful; those whose scales are light have lost their souls\" (23:102-103). A Muslim learns that no deed is too small to count, and that Allah's weighing is perfectly just.",
"faith_032": "The verse belongs to Surah Al-Kahf. Just before it, the surah describes those whose effort in this life was wasted (18:103-105). Then comes the contrast: \"Indeed, those who have believed and done righteous deeds, they will have the Gardens of Firdaws as a lodging, abiding therein forever; they will not desire any change from it\" (18:107-108). Surah Al-Mu'minun promises the same to the humble believers: \"They will inherit al-Firdaws; they will abide therein forever\" (23:10-11). A Muslim learns what earns Firdaws: faith joined to good deeds.",
"faith_033": "The name appears in Surah Al 'Imran: \"Say to those who disbelieve: You will be overcome and gathered to Jahannam, and wretched is the resting place\" (3:12). The Qur'an gives the Fire other names, such as Saqar (74:26-27), Laza (70:15) and al-Hutamah (104:4), but Jahannam returns most often. Surah Maryam says that everyone will come to it, then Allah saves those who feared Him and leaves the wrongdoers in it (19:71-72). A Muslim learns these names as warnings that keep him on the path, not as a cause for despair.",
"faith_034": "The verse belongs to Surah Al-Qamar, which describes the wrongdoers dragged into the Fire and told to taste the touch of Saqar (54:47-48). Then: \"Indeed, all things We created with measure\" (54:49). The surah adds that every small and great thing people did is written down (54:52-53). Surah Al-Hadid says no disaster strikes the earth or ourselves except that it is in a register before Allah brings it about (57:22). A Muslim learns to meet what happens with patience, knowing it was measured and known by Allah before it came.",
"faith_035": "The verse belongs to Surah An-Nisa: \"Indeed, Allah does not forgive that partners be associated with Him, but He forgives what is less than that for whom He wills. And whoever associates others with Allah has certainly fabricated a tremendous sin\" (4:48). Luqman gives the same warning to his son: \"O my son, do not associate anything with Allah; indeed, association is a great injustice\" (31:13). A Muslim learns to guard the oneness of Allah above everything, and to turn back to Him while there is time.",
"faith_036": "The verse is in Surah Al-Baqarah, closing an answer about purity in married life with a general truth: \"Indeed, Allah loves those who constantly repent, and He loves those who purify themselves\" (2:222). For those who repent, believe and do good, Allah replaces their evil deeds with good ones (25:70). He calls the believers to \"repent to Allah with sincere repentance\" (66:8). A Muslim learns that a fault followed by sincere return does not push him away from Allah; the returning itself is what Allah loves.",
"faith_037": "In Surah Nuh, the prophet Nuh tells his Lord how his people fled from his call night and day (71:5-9). He recounts his words to them: \"Ask forgiveness of your Lord; indeed, He is ever Forgiving. He will send the sky upon you in abundant rain, and give you wealth and children, gardens and rivers\" (71:10-12). The prophet Hud promised his people the same (11:52). A Muslim learns that asking forgiveness is not only for the Hereafter; it opens the blessings of this life too.",
"faith_038": "The verse is in Surah At-Talaq, among rules on divorce: \"And whoever fears Allah, He will make for him a way out, and will provide for him from where he does not expect\" (65:2). \"And whoever relies upon Allah, then He is sufficient for him. Indeed, Allah will accomplish His purpose\" (65:3). Believers facing an enemy said, \"Allah is sufficient for us, the best Disposer of affairs\" (3:173). A Muslim learns that reliance follows effort and fear of Allah, and that the outcome then rests in His hands.",
"faith_039": "The verse belongs to Surah Ghafir: \"And your Lord says: Call upon Me; I will respond to you. Indeed, those who are too proud to worship Me will enter Jahannam humbled\" (40:60). Supplication is placed inside worship itself, and turning away from it is called pride. Surah Al-Baqarah gives the same promise: \"Indeed I am near; I respond to the call of the caller when he calls upon Me\" (2:186). A Muslim learns that du'a is never a bother to Allah; He commands it, and He answers it.",
"faith_040": "The verse belongs to Surah Ar-Ra'd, after Allah says that He guides to Himself whoever turns back to Him (13:27). Then He describes those who turn: \"Those who believe and whose hearts find rest in the remembrance of Allah. Unquestionably, by the remembrance of Allah do hearts find rest\" (13:28). Surah Al-Ahzab tells believers to remember Allah much, morning and evening (33:41-42), and Allah says, \"Remember Me; I will remember you\" (2:152). A Muslim learns that peace of heart is not found by chasing it but by remembering Allah.",
"faith_041": "Fajr is the prayer of dawn, and the Qur'an names it: it speaks of \"the prayer of Fajr\" and \"the prayer of 'Isha\" in one verse (24:58), and of \"the recitation of dawn\" that is witnessed (17:78). The same verse orders prayer \"from the decline of the sun until the darkness of the night\" (17:78), the span that holds Dhuhr, 'Asr, Maghrib and 'Isha. Anyone can verify the order from a mosque's five daily calls. A Muslim begins each day with Fajr, opening the day with Allah.",
"faith_042": "A rak'ah is one unit of the prayer: standing with recitation, bowing, rising, and two prostrations with a sitting between them. Fajr has two, the shortest of the five; with Dhuhr, 'Asr and 'Isha at four each and Maghrib at three, a day's obligatory prayers add up to seventeen rak'ahs. The count is the same in every mosque. The Qur'an calls the dawn recitation \"witnessed\" (17:78), and it is recited aloud in both rak'ahs. A Muslim learns Fajr first, as the prayer easiest to master.",
"faith_043": "Maghrib is prayed once the sun has set, and it alone among the obligatory prayers has three rak'ahs, the first two recited aloud and the third silently. Every Muslim community prays it this way. The Qur'an frames these times: prayer \"at the two ends of the day and in the early hours of the night\" (11:114). In Ramadan the fast is completed \"until the night\" (2:187), and Maghrib marks the moment of breaking it. A Muslim learns Maghrib as the prayer that closes the day's work.",
"faith_044": "The adhan is the public call announcing that a prayer's time has come. It begins with \"Allahu Akbar\", carries the two testimonies that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad ﷺ is His Messenger, invites, \"Come to prayer, come to success,\" and ends with \"La ilaha illa Allah.\" The Qur'an refers to it: \"When the call to prayer is made on the day of Jumu'ah, hasten to the remembrance of Allah and leave trade\" (62:9). A Muslim learns to answer the adhan by heading to prayer.",
"faith_045": "The verse is in Surah Al-Ma'idah: \"O you who believe, when you rise for prayer, wash your faces and your hands up to the elbows, wipe your heads, and wash your feet up to the ankles\" (5:6). Elbows and ankles are named so the limit is clear. The verse ends with the reason: \"Allah does not intend to make difficulty for you, but He intends to purify you and complete His favour upon you\" (5:6). A Muslim learns that wudu is a purification Allah counts among His favours.",
"faith_046": "Surah Al-Ma'idah gives the allowance in the verse that teaches ablution: \"If you are ill or on a journey… and you find no water, then seek clean earth and wipe your faces and your hands with it. Allah does not intend to make difficulty for you\" (5:6). Surah An-Nisa gives the same permission (4:43). The ill and the traveller are named first, and the prayer is not dropped for them but made possible. A Muslim learns that Allah makes His worship reachable in every circumstance.",
"faith_047": "The word khutba means an address. On Friday the imam delivers it from the minbar in two parts, while the congregation listens in silence; the prayer of two rak'ahs follows. The Qur'an says: \"When the call to prayer is made on the day of Jumu'ah, hasten to the remembrance of Allah and leave trade\" (62:9), and after the prayer, \"disperse in the land and seek of the bounty of Allah\" (62:10). A Muslim learns to arrive before the khutba and to listen as part of the worship.",
"faith_048": "Zakat on savings is a quarter of a tenth (rub' al-'ushr): for every hundred kept above the threshold for a lunar year, two and a half are given. The rate is the same in every book of Islamic law. The Qur'an pairs zakat with prayer, as in \"establish prayer and give zakat\" (2:43), and names its eight recipients, from the poor and the needy to the debtor and the stranded traveller (9:60). A Muslim learns to count his savings each year and set aside Allah's share.",
"faith_049": "The hadith is narrated by Ibn 'Umar in Sahih al-Bukhari 1503: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made zakat al-fitr obligatory, a sa' of dates or of barley, on slave and free, male and female, young and old among the Muslims, to be paid before the people go out to the prayer. The Qur'an, closing the verses on fasting, tells believers to magnify Allah for having guided them (2:185). A Muslim gives zakat al-fitr for each member of the household before going out to the Eid prayer.",
"faith_050": "The verse belongs to the passage of Surah Al-Baqarah that lays down the fast of Ramadan, the month in which the Qur'an was sent down (2:185). It sets the day's limits: \"Eat and drink until the white thread of dawn becomes distinct to you from the black thread; then complete the fast until the night\" (2:187). The white thread is the first light along the horizon. A Muslim learns that the fast has exact edges, and that eating before dawn and breaking at nightfall both obey the verse.",
"faith_051": "The hadith is narrated by Anas ibn Malik in Sahih al-Bukhari 1923: the Prophet ﷺ said, \"Take the suhur, for indeed there is blessing in the suhur.\" The Qur'an allows eating and drinking \"until the white thread of dawn becomes distinct to you from the black thread\" (2:187), so the meal fits the time the verse leaves open. A Muslim rises before dawn in Ramadan for the suhur, even for a few dates and water, because the Prophet ﷺ called it a blessing.",
"faith_052": "The word iftar comes from the root that means to break, and the same root gives Eid al-Fitr its name: the festival of breaking the fast. The Qur'an fixes the moment: \"then complete the fast until the night\" (2:187), and night begins when the sun sets, so the fasting person eats as soon as the sun is down, then prays Maghrib. A Muslim learns that the fast has an appointed end each day, and that breaking it on time is itself obedience, as holding it was.",
"faith_053": "Eid al-Fitr falls on 1 Shawwal, the day after Ramadan ends; Eid al-Adha on 10 Dhul-Hijja, while the pilgrims are at Mina. Each opens with a prayer of two rak'ahs and the takbir, \"Allahu Akbar\" repeated aloud. The Qur'an ties both to worship: at the end of the fast believers \"magnify Allah for having guided you\" (2:185), and in the pilgrimage days they mention Allah's name over the animals He provided (22:28). A Muslim learns that an Eid is a day of thanks to Allah, prayer before feast.",
"faith_054": "Surah As-Saffat tells the story. Ibrahim was given a forbearing boy; when the boy could work beside him, he told him of a dream of sacrificing him, and the son answered, \"O my father, do as you are commanded; you will find me, if Allah wills, of the steadfast\" (37:102). When both submitted, Allah called, \"O Ibrahim, you have fulfilled the vision\" (37:104-105), and then: \"And We ransomed him with a great sacrifice\" (37:107). A Muslim learns that Eid al-Adha recalls a father and son who put Allah's command first.",
"faith_055": "Dhul-Hijja is the twelfth month of the lunar year, and its name means \"the one of the pilgrimage\". The Qur'an says that \"the hajj is in well-known months\" (2:197) and that pilgrimage to the House is a duty owed to Allah by those able to reach it (3:97). The rites fill the month's second week: Mina on the eighth, 'Arafat on the ninth, Eid al-Adha on the tenth, and the stoning on the days after. A Muslim learns to place the hajj at the very end of the lunar year.",
"faith_056": "The standing at 'Arafat fills the afternoon of 9 Dhul-Hijja: from midday until sunset the pilgrims stay on the plain, praying and asking forgiveness, and there is no hajj without it. The Qur'an mentions the departure from it: \"And when you pour forth from 'Arafat, remember Allah at the Sacred Monument\" (2:198), the stop at Muzdalifa for that night. Every pilgrim stands on the same day; the hajj itself verifies the date. A Muslim learns that the heart of the hajj is a day spent standing before Allah.",
"faith_057": "The tawaf is seven circuits, counted from the corner of the Black Stone, with the Ka'ba kept on the pilgrim's left; pilgrims then pray two rak'ahs behind the Station of Ibrahim. Allah charged Ibrahim and Isma'il to purify His House \"for those who perform tawaf, those who stay for worship, and those who bow and prostrate\" (2:125), and He commands the pilgrims to \"circle the Ancient House\" (22:29). A Muslim learns to count the seven quietly, remembering that circling the House is worship of its Lord alone.",
"faith_058": "The sa'y is walked between the hills of Safa and Marwa: Safa to Marwa is one lap, back to Safa the second, and the seventh ends at Marwa. The Qur'an says: \"Indeed, Safa and Marwa are among the symbols of Allah; whoever makes hajj to the House or performs 'umra, there is no blame on him for going between them\" (2:158). Ibrahim settled his family in this valley (14:37). A Muslim walks in the footsteps of Hajar, learning that effort and trust in Allah go together.",
"faith_059": "Ihram begins at a set boundary on the way to Makkah, with the intention and the talbiya; men put on two unsewn white cloths, women ordinary modest dress. The Qur'an names what the state forbids: \"there is no intimacy, no wrongdoing and no quarrelling during the hajj\" (2:197), and \"do not kill game while you are in ihram\" (5:95). It ends when the pilgrim shaves or cuts the hair. A Muslim learns that ihram strips away rank: every pilgrim, rich or poor, stands before Allah in the same cloths.",
"faith_060": "The hadith is narrated by Ibn 'Umar in Sahih al-Bukhari 1549, which completes the talbiya of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: \"inna al-hamda wan-ni'mata laka wal-mulk, la sharika lak\": indeed all praise, favour and sovereignty are Yours; You have no partner. It answers the call Allah gave Ibrahim: \"Proclaim the hajj to the people; they will come to you on foot and on every lean camel\" (22:27). A Muslim learns the talbiya as the pilgrim's voice: he has come, and Allah has no partner.",
"faith_061": "Three stone pillars stand at Mina: the small, the middle and the great, called Jamrat al-'Aqaba. On 10 Dhul-Hijja pilgrims throw seven pebbles at the great pillar only, saying \"Allahu Akbar\" with each; on the following days they stone all three, seven each. The Qur'an speaks of numbered days of remembering Allah, in which leaving after two days or staying longer are both allowed (2:203). A Muslim learns that the stoning is a remembrance of Allah, done in order and with the takbir, not a show of anger.",
"faith_062": "The Black Stone sits in the corner of the Ka'ba where every tawaf starts and every circuit is counted; a pilgrim passing it touches or kisses it, or points toward it saying \"Allahu Akbar\". The Qur'an tells of the House, raised by Ibrahim and Isma'il (2:127), and of Allah's charge: \"Do not associate anything with Me, and purify My House for those who perform tawaf\" (22:26). A Muslim learns that the Stone marks the start of a worship offered to Allah alone; it is honoured, never worshipped.",
"faith_063": "The kiswa is black silk, with a wide belt around its upper part and a curtain over the door, both embroidered in gold thread with verses of the Qur'an. A new one is made each year. The Qur'an calls the house it covers \"the Ka'ba, the Sacred House\", made by Allah \"a standing for people\" (5:97), and \"the first House established for mankind\" (3:96). A Muslim learns that the kiswa honours the House, whose sanctity comes from Allah's choice, not from any cloth.",
"faith_064": "The 'umra is the shorter visit: ihram, tawaf, sa'y, then shaving or cutting the hair; there is no standing at 'Arafat and no stoning at Mina. The Qur'an names both journeys together: \"Complete the hajj and the 'umra for Allah\" (2:196). Surah Al-Fath promises the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions such a visit: \"You will surely enter the Sacred Mosque, if Allah wills, in safety, with heads shaved and hair shortened\" (48:27). A Muslim learns that the 'umra fits any month, while the hajj has its fixed days.",
"faith_065": "The name comes at the end of Surah Al-Hashr, after a series of Allah's names (59:22-23): \"He is Allah, the Creator, the Originator, the Fashioner; to Him belong the best names. Whatever is in the heavens and the earth glorifies Him\" (59:24). Surah Al-A'raf tells what to do with these names: \"To Allah belong the best names, so call upon Him by them\" (7:180). A Muslim learns to use the name Al-Khaliq in praise and in prayer, seeing every creature as His work.",
"faith_066": "The name closes a verse of Surah Az-Zumar: \"Say: O My servants who have transgressed against themselves, do not despair of the mercy of Allah. Indeed, Allah forgives all sins; indeed, He is the Forgiving, the Merciful\" (39:53). The next verse tells what to do with that hope: \"Turn back to your Lord and submit to Him, before the punishment comes upon you\" (39:54). A Muslim learns to say Al-Ghafur as a reason never to give up on himself, and to turn back while the door is open.",
"faith_067": "The two names close the verse of the Night Journey: \"Exalted is He who took His servant by night from the Sacred Mosque to the Farthest Mosque, whose surroundings We have blessed. Indeed, He is the Hearing, the Seeing\" (17:1). The same pair comes when Allah reassures Musa and Harun before Pharaoh: \"Fear not; indeed, I am with you both, I hear and I see\" (20:46). A Muslim learns that nothing said or done, by night or day, escapes Allah, a comfort to the one who calls on Him.",
"faith_068": "The name is in Surah Al-Ikhlas: \"Say: He is Allah, the One\" (112:1), \"Allah, as-Samad\" (112:2), \"He neither begets nor is begotten, and there is none equal to Him\" (112:3-4). The verses around the name explain it: He has no origin and no offspring, and nothing equals Him. Surah Fatir says the same: \"you are the ones in need of Allah, while Allah is the Free of need, the Praiseworthy\" (35:15). A Muslim learns to bring every need to As-Samad, who alone needs nothing and is needed by all.",
"faith_069": "The Throne Verse in Surah Al-Baqarah opens with these names: \"Allah, there is no god but Him, the Ever-Living, the Self-Sustaining. Neither drowsiness overtakes Him nor sleep\" (2:255). The verse goes on: to Him belongs all in the heavens and the earth, His Kursi extends over them, and guarding them does not tire Him (2:255). Allah also says, \"Rely upon the Ever-Living who does not die\" (25:58). A Muslim learns that the One he relies on never sleeps, never dies and never tires of watching over him.",
"faith_070": "The verse belongs to Surah Adh-Dhariyat, where Allah states why He created us: \"I did not create the jinn and mankind except to worship Me. I do not want from them any provision, nor do I want them to feed Me\" (51:56-57). Then: \"Indeed, it is Allah who is the Provider, the Possessor of strength, the Firm\" (51:58). Surah Hud says no creature moves on the earth but Allah provides for it (11:6). A Muslim learns to seek his livelihood with effort and without anxiety, trusting Ar-Razzaq.",
}
